# CPU affinity configuration for the ARC++ container — x86_64 (amd64) boards.
#
# Overrides fydroid-rt-common/init/init.cpusets.rc, which narrows the background
# cpusets for RK3588S's 4x A55 + 4x A76 big.LITTLE split ("Android gets the
# little cores; Chrome OS keeps the big cores"). amd64 hosts are SMP-uniform:
# there is no little cluster to bank work onto, so narrowing does not hand
# anything back to Chrome OS, it just pins Android to a subset of identical
# CPUs. Measured on device (i3-1005G1, 2 cores / 4 threads) the rt-common masks
# put every background app, every restricted process and every lmkd-forced app
# restart on cpu0 alone, and all system services on one physical core.
#
# So keep AOSP's own default: every cpuset gets every CPU. Copying from the
# cpuset root rather than writing a literal mask keeps this correct on a
# 2-thread box and a 32-thread one alike — system/core/rootdir/init.rc:335-361
# does exactly this at `on init`, leaving it to the device init.rc to narrow it
# or leave it alone. We leave it alone.
#
# Background work is still deprioritised, by cpu.shares in the background
# scheduling group: proportional, and it yields the CPU under contention
# instead of being fenced off from it while cores sit idle. That is the right
# lever on a uniform SMP host; a hard cpuset mask is not.

on post-stune
    write /dev/stune/foreground/cpu.uclamp.min 20
    write /dev/stune/foreground/cpu.uclamp.latency_sensitive 1

on property:sys.boot_completed=1
    copy /dev/cpuset/cpus /dev/cpuset/foreground/cpus
    copy /dev/cpuset/cpus /dev/cpuset/background/cpus
    copy /dev/cpuset/cpus /dev/cpuset/system-background/cpus
    copy /dev/cpuset/cpus /dev/cpuset/restricted/cpus
    copy /dev/cpuset/cpus /dev/cpuset/top-app/cpus
